-- title:
--   Taylor division by X₁-φ(X₀) in two-variable power series
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be a commutative ring, let $\varphi \in k[[T]]$ be a one-variable formal power series whose constant coefficient vanishes, and let $G \in k[[X_0,X_1]]$ be a formal power series in two variables, indexed by `Fin 2`. Since $X_0$ has zero constant coefficient, the substitution $\varphi(X_0) :=$ `PowerSeries.subst (X 0) φ` is defined in $k[[X_0,X_1]]$, and since $\varphi(X_0)$ likewise has zero constant coefficient, the two-variable substitution sending $X_0 \mapsto X_0$ and $X_1 \mapsto \varphi(X_0)$ is defined. The assertion is that there exists $Q \in k[[X_0,X_1]]$ with
--   $$G = \bigl(X_1 - \varphi(X_0)\bigr)\, Q + G\bigl(X_0,\varphi(X_0)\bigr),$$
--   where the last term is the image of $G$ under the substitution $![X_0, \varphi(X_0)]$. Thus division with remainder by $X_1 - \varphi(X_0)$ is possible, the remainder being the restriction of $G$ to the graph $X_1 = \varphi(X_0)$; no uniqueness of $Q$ is claimed.
--
--   This is the Taylor (Weierstrass-type) division of a two-variable power series by the distinguished element $X_1 - \varphi(X_0)$ cutting out the graph of $\varphi$; it yields in particular that the kernel of $k[[X_0,X_1]] \to k[[T]]$, $X_0 \mapsto T$, $X_1 \mapsto \varphi(T)$, is generated by $X_1 - \varphi(X_0)$. It is used by [`MvPowerSeries.exists_eq_X_sub_subst_mul_of_subst_eq_zero`](thm.html#MvPowerSeries.exists_eq_X_sub_subst_mul_of_subst_eq_zero) and, through it, by [`MvPowerSeries.isReduced_quotient_span_of_sub_drinfeld_mem_pow`](thm.html#MvPowerSeries.isReduced_quotient_span_of_sub_drinfeld_mem_pow).
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_MvPowerSeries_exists_eq_X_sub_subst_mul_add_subst_of_constantCoeff_eq_zero.lean

import Mathlib

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
set_option synthInstance.maxHeartbeats 400000
